<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lex Flavia Malacitana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 81–84</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lex Flavia Malacitana is the municipal charter of Malaca (Málaga) in the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> Baetica, issued under the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> around 81–84 CE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> tablets bearing it and the charter of nearby Salpensa were found near Málaga in 1851. Its rules on elections and magistrates are a major source for Roman town government in the provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lex-flavia-malacitana.jpg" alt="Lex Flavia Malacitana" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stgoetz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lex_malacitana_ch_56.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Malaca came under Roman rule peacefully, through agreements of mutual friendship and equality, as much of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-ulterior">Hispania Ulterior</a> did. As the Municipium Malacitanum it was a hub of trade on the Via Herculea, linked to the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> and to other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> ports. After the civil wars of 68-69, Vespasian founded the Flavian dynasty and extended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> rights to all of Hispania; the city may have asked for its Flavian law in 74, but the grant came only under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a>, who is named in the oaths of the text. Together with the laws of Salpensa and Irni it gives the fullest surviving version of the Flavian municipal law.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The surviving bronze, one of what were originally five tablets, measures about 89 by 122 cm and weighs 90 kg, and holds chapters 51 to 66. Such tablets stood in the forum so that citizens could know the town's rules. Its provisions concern the duoviri iuri dicundo and the election of magistrates by popular assemblies, the choice of municipal patrons and the handling of public funds, with rights and duties graded by wealth. Owners of destroyed buildings had to rebuild within a year or pay a fine, and citizens could put forward their own candidates for aedile. Traces of white paint remain on the letters, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a>'s name was erased after the Senate condemned his memory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Local workers found the tablets at Monte de El Ejido in Málaga and meant to sell them as scrap to the Luque family of bronze craftsmen. Before they could be melted, the Málaga couple Amalia Heredia Livermore and Jorge Loring Oyarzábal bought them for a collection, and Manuel Rodríguez de Berlanga y Rosado studied and translated the text, for which he received the Order of Isabella the Catholic. Shown for years in the Loringiano Museum on the La Concepción estate, the bronzes were sold to the state and are now in the National Archaeological Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. A petition of December 2016 to return them to the Museo de Málaga was refused by the Spanish government on 12 March 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lex Malacitana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As Malaca it received municipal status under the Flavian emperors, recorded in the bronze <b>Lex Flavia Malacitana</b>, and continued as a major port of Hispania Baetica.</blockquote><a href="/state/malaka">Malaka</a></li></ul></section>
